iately.</summary><category term="News" /></entry><entry><title>Surrogacy in Georgia is booming, but at what cost?</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:25230025a4f9392e3103507d2ee1619ab7e2d7f78c66b92f0d7a48d6773661fa</id><updated>2026-10-11T20:25:19Z</updated><published>2026-10-11T07:45:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/surrogacy-in-georgia-is-booming-but-at-what-cost/a-79629971--321e2c4c57d1.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/surrogacy-in-georgia-is-booming-but-at-what-cost/a-79629971?maca=en-rss-en-world-4025-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">Georgia became a hub for surrogate pregnancies after Russia's full-scale invasion of Ukraine in 2022. DW spoke with parents, and those who wanted to become parents, about the hidden costs of a booming industry.</summary><category term="World" /></entry><entry><title>Money, networks and bias: Why women struggle to win seats in US politics</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:a673485131aa22308e9e8a4dc2b9018c53a540ffd6652bfc0a9dd81a4426d948</id><updated>2026-10-11T20:24:09Z</updated><published>2026-10-10T19:16:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/money-networks-and-bias-why-women-struggle-to-win-seats-in-us-politics/a-79625179--d768d0a80615.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/money-networks-and-bias-why-women-struggle-to-win-seats-in-us-politics/a-79625179?maca=en-rss-en-world-4025-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">The US is holding Congress and state governor elections. At all levels of US politics, women are underrepresented — but they may win some historic victories in the midterms on November 3.</summary><category term="World" /></entry><entry><title>Is China seeking to restrict Taiwan's cultural presence?</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:ad5ade01683cb006ca615bc7975ab62c22554c724416c62e37020db56ed6a15b</id><updated>2026-10-11T20:24:09Z</updated><published>2026-10-10T06:19:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href